The rwlock API is exposed on a GNU host only when __USE_UNIX98 or
__USE_XOPEN2K is defined. Those are glibc's own internal macros, set by its
features.h; a C library that is not glibc defines neither, so the whole
rwlock block is dropped from npth.h while npth.c still defines the
functions, and the build stops on an unknown npth_rwlock_t.

Keying the guard on __GLIBC__ as well leaves glibc exactly as it was and
exposes the API on every other library, which is what the musl case already
does.

--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -399,7 +399,7 @@
         INSERT_EXPOSE_RWLOCK_API="1"
         ;;
     *-*-linux-gnu*|*-*-gnu*)
-        INSERT_EXPOSE_RWLOCK_API="defined(__USE_UNIX98) || defined(__USE_XOPEN2K)"
+        INSERT_EXPOSE_RWLOCK_API="!defined(__GLIBC__) || defined(__USE_UNIX98) || defined(__USE_XOPEN2K)"
         ;;
     *)
         INSERT_EXPOSE_RWLOCK_API="1"
